[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  wednesday august 10th  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  during the pandemic demand for software  e-commerce video games and pretty much  anything with the word crypto in it went  into overdrive  that led tech companies to hire new  workers at a rapid clip  but now times are changing  high inflation and continued supply  chain disruptions are causing economic  growth to slow and taking hiring with it  in just the last few months crypto  exchange coinbase let go of 18 of its  workforce tesla laid off 200 people  netflix said it was cutting three  percent of its staff  and companies like google parent  alphabet amazon and facebook parent meta  have all said they will slow their  hiring plans  not to mention the cuts happening at  startups  so is this a sign of doom for the tech  labor market  my guest today says maybe not  zeta howard is a partner and the chief  talent officer for venture capital firm  ivp  the firm has invested in companies like  dropbox github and snap and its current  portfolio includes discord and grammarly  hi sata thanks for joining me thank you  happy to be here  so start off by telling me a little bit  about what your role is  so my role at ivp is to help our  portfolio companies with all things  related to  talent human capital essentially the  people in their organization sometimes  it's around helping them recruit in  senior level executives sometimes it's  around figuring out how to face a  worldwide pandemic sometimes it's about  thinking about returned office  strategies hiring strategies in general  but basically anything related to  their employees  i want to talk about a few of those  things that you just mentioned because  the the landscape for hiring in tech  right now is is changing we're hearing  about a lot of layoffs and hiring  freezes you know from your perspective  what does that say about kind of where  the industry is right now  so i think the interesting thing to note  about that is that this is i think very  different than layoffs hiring freezes  from the past  i would call this strategic cuts  strategic freeze versus what we saw back  in 2001 2000 with the technology and the  dot-com bust where it was very desperate  companies were going out of business  they were no longer going to exist if  they didn't make some of these cuts or  even if you think about 2008 and 2009  the worldwide financial meltdown this is  very different in that companies  throughout this year throughout 2022  have been looking at their runway for  the future and thinking about how do we  preserve the cash that we've raised over  the past two to three years and so  they're making very difficult decisions  around the people their organization  they're maybe looking at new initiatives  that they started that aren't going to  be successful and cutting those  initiatives they're thinking much more  critically around growth i think in the  last few years it's been growth and  growth at all costs which meant hiring  as many people as you could for whatever  salaries you could get them at the same  time i think it's important to note that  even while companies are making cuts or  maybe putting on freezes there are still  key roles that they're hiring for and  there are still jobs to be had within  the technology landscape  what's behind that pullback though why  does tech in particular find itself in  this place i think tech in particular  finds itself in this place because the  growth has just been so astronomical  throughout the pandemic if you think  about how many people had heard the word  zoom and knew what zoom was in january  of 2020 we did as an organization we  were using zoom for meetings etc not to  the extent that we are now versus august  of 2020 it's a verb now it's it's  commonplace everything that we do and  that filters through technology in all  different sorts of ways you've got to  think about how you protect your  organization from a security standpoint  you've got to think about how you're  working together from a collaboration  standpoint and so many different ways  and so many different touch points  technology has really benefited frankly  from the pandemic in ways that were  unforeseen before and that doesn't go on  forever so i think because the highs  were so high it feels like the lows are  so lows when what is normal which is  things slow down and the come back down  is happening okay but it it seems like  kind of because these highs have have  been so high we're hearing a lot of ceos  say you know we over hired we extended  ourselves too much  to an extent you hear something like  that and you think like well as an  employee that's that's not my fault that  sounds like bad management you know and  yet the individuals kind of end up  having to deal with those repercussions  exactly individuals do end up having to  deal with the repercussions i think it's  easy to say it's bad management and i  think with some companies that's  certainly a part of it it's also no one  can really predict the future with all  certainty there are certainly ceos that  have done a  better job of understanding their  marketplace and maybe  for good or for bad or more conservative  in their growth strategies were more  conservative and how they were bringing  people on and how many but they were  also maybe missing part of the market  and part of market share that they  should have been getting and could have  been getting but now when they look back  they maybe seem smarter in this  particular moment because they're making  fewer cuts and then there were those  that were very aggressive that were  maybe doing better raising money at  higher evaluations growing even faster  that have to make even bigger swings the  other way because of that it's it's hard  you know hindsight is absolutely 20 20  on these types of things and it's hard  to say right wrong i think the other  thing to remember is  it isn't over right this is just a point  in time this is a marker and if you did  that marker for every single company  there was a time where they were at a  high there were a time where they were  maybe at a low or at a medium the  conversation still continues but you're  absolutely right employees are the one  that end up bearing the brunt of these  things  i want to ask you about something that  feels a little unique to the situation  we have at the moment and you're seeing  these big tech companies ceos come out  and say things like you know if you're  not good enough then maybe you should  decide to leave our startups and smaller  companies taking that kind of approach  to and what does that do to the market  that is a great question and i can tell  you  in talking to some of our portfolio ceos  i have not heard that sort of  positioning i have not heard that  statement around if you're not good  enough et cetera and this is just my  personal opinion i think with maybe some  of the larger companies because when you  get to 10 000 employees etc there is a  little bit more of an ability to ride  the coattails and hide maybe in certain  areas and maybe you don't have to to be  at that top ten percent top five percent  all the time there's a there's a larger  bell curve if you will when you're at a  smaller startup when you're at 200  employees when you're one of  five customer success reps not even just  there's no place to hide but there's so  much work to be done with fewer  resources that you really are  most of the time operating at such a  high level i don't know if it makes  sense in the same way  all right that was sada howard chief  talent officer for ivp  thanks for joining us thank you happy to  be here  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

